// D1 round 5 / H6 — WHERE A CALLEE'S BODY LIVES SHOULD BE INERT.
//
// This is pass/bc_d1r5_h6_cross_archive_admits' leaking twin — mutate while
// the stored borrow is still readable — with ONE thing changed: `stash2`'s
// body is in this file instead of in libbcxa.a. It REFUSES, because the
// borrow-flow summary is computed over a body this LProgram has.
//
// The cross-archive spelling of the same program is ADMITTED (measured on this
// tree; the archived body arrives as an unmaterialized Writ ExternalRef, so
// body_available() answers false and the flow dump prints `UNAVAILABLE`). Only
// this half is pinned: a `.expected` asserting that the other one compiles
// would record the defect as intended, and the leak is a residual with a named
// class, not a behaviour to lock in.
//
// ⚠ THE CORRECTION THIS FIXTURE CARRIES. The residual list used to name
// `from_binary_module()` as item (b) and D1 round 5 DELETED it as "false — not
// a hole", citing a measurement against a real archive in which the leak
// refused. That measurement does not reproduce: re-run here against both the
// original probe archive and a freshly rebuilt one, the summary is UNAVAILABLE
// and the leak compiles rc=0. The predicate claim was right (body_available
// never tests from_binary_module) and the CONCLUSION was wrong — an archived
// body lands in class (c) instead. Cheap fixes are ruled out by the arena: an
// external body must be walked through the FOREIGN arena and its FOREIGN type
// pool, which is the cross-arena arc, not a gate tweak.
//
// So: this fixture is the closed edge, and the open edge is written down.
// Repro for the open edge (both halves, one command each):
//   logosc --emit-module tests/logos/bc_cross_archive/bcxa.module -o /tmp/libbcxa.a
//   LOGOS_DUMP_FLOWS=stash2 logosc <the mutate-after twin> -l /tmp/libbcxa.a
package bc_d1r5_h6_local_body_twin;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
#[borrow_carrying]
struct B { p: &i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }
impl C {
    fn mk(&self) -> B { return B { p: &self.v }; }
    fn bump(&mut self) { self.v = self.v + 1i64; }
}
fn stash2(c: &C, v: &mut Vec<B>) { v.push(c.mk()); }
fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let mut vs: Vec<B> = Vec::new();
    stash2(&c, &mut vs);
    c.bump();
    return *vs.get(0u64).p;
}
